#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:114
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:117
msgid ""
"This section aims to introduce any relevant details about the standard "
"library and compiler that are assumed knowledge by referenced sources and "
"later RFCs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:121
msgid ""
"See [_Implementation summary_](#implementation-summary) for a summary of the "
"current unstable build-std feature in Cargo."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:124
msgid "Standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:127
msgid ""
"Since the first stable release of Rust, the standard library has been "
"distributed as a pre-built artifact via rustup, which has a variety of "
"advantages and/or rationale:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:131
msgid ""
"It saves Rust users from having to rebuild the standard library whenever "
"they start a project or do a clean build"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:133
msgid ""
"The standard library has and has had dependencies which require a more "
"complicated build environment than typical Rust projects"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:135
msgid ""
"e.g. requiring a working C toolchain to build `compiler_builtins`' `c` "
"feature"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:137
msgid ""
"To varying degrees at different times in its development, the standard "
"library's implementation has been tied to the compiler implementation and "
"has had to change in lockstep"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:141
msgid ""
"Not all targets have a pre-built standard library distributed via rustup, "
"though it is a minimum requirement for certain platform support tiers. "
"According to rustc's [platform support docs](https://doc.rust-lang.org/"
"nightly/rustc/platform-support.html), for tier three targets:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:145
msgid ""
"Tier 3 targets are those which the Rust codebase has support for, but which "
"the Rust project does not build or test automatically, so they may or may "
"not work. Official builds are not available."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:149
msgid "..and tier two targets:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:151
msgid ""
"The Rust project builds official binary releases of the standard library "
"(or, in some cases, only the core library) for each tier 2 target, and "
"automated builds ensure that each tier 2 target can be used as build target "
"after each change."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:156
msgid "..and finally, tier one targets:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:158
msgid ""
"The Rust project builds official binary releases for each tier 1 target, and "
"automated testing ensures that each tier 1 target builds and passes tests "
"after each change."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:162
msgid ""
"As an innate property of the target, not all targets can support the `std` "
"crate. This is independent of its tier, where as stated in the [Target Tier "
"Policy](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/rustc/target-tier-policy.html) "
"lower-tier targets may not have a complete implementation for all APIs in "
"the crates they can support."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:167
msgid ""
"All of the standard library crates leverage permanently unstable features "
"provided by the compiler that will never be stabilised and therefore require "
"nightly to build."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:171
msgid ""
"The configuration for the pre-built standard library build is spread across "
"bootstrap, the standard library workspace, individual standard library crate "
"manifests and the target specification. The pre-built standard library is "
"installed into the sysroot."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:176
msgid ""
"At the beginning of compilation, unless the crate has the `#![no_std]` "
"attribute, the compiler will load the `libstd.rlib` file from the sysroot as "
"a dependency of the current crate and add an implicit `extern crate std` for "
"it. This is the mechanism by which every crate has an implicit dependency on "
"the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:182
msgid ""
"The standard library sources are distributed in the `rust-src` component by "
"rustup and placed in the sysroot under `lib/rustlib/src/`. The sources "
"consist of the `library/` workspace plus `src/llvm-project/libunwind`, which "
"was required in the past to build the `unwind` crate on some targets."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:187
msgid ""
"Cargo supports explicitly declaring a dependency on crates with the same "
"names as standard library crates with a `path` source (e.g. `core = { path = "
"\"../my_core\" }`), which rustc will load instead of crates in the sysroot. "
"Crates with these dependencies are not accepted by crates.io, but there are "
"crates on GitHub that use this pattern, such as [embed-rs/stm32f7-discovery]"
"(https://github.com/embed-rs/stm32f7-discovery/blob/"
"e2bf713263791c028c2a897f2eb1830d7f09eceb/Cargo.toml#L21), which are used as "
"`git` dependencies of other crates on GitHub."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:195
msgid "Dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:198
msgid ""
"Behind the facade, the standard library is split into multiple crates, some "
"of which are in different repositories and included as submodules or using "
"[JOSH](https://josh-project.github.io/josh/intro.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:201
msgid ""
"As well as local crates, the standard library depends on crates from crates."
"io. It needs to be able to point these crates' dependencies on the standard "
"library at the sources of `core`, `alloc` and `std` in the current [rust-"
"lang/rust](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust) checkout."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:206
msgid ""
"This is achieved through use of the `rustc-dep-of-std` feature. Crates used "
"in the dependency graph of `std` declare a `rustc-dep-of-std` feature and "
"when enabled, add new dependencies on `rustc-std-workspace-{core,alloc,std}"
"`. `rustc-std-workspace-{core,alloc,std}` are empty crates published on "
"crates.io. As part of the workspace for the standard library, `rustc-std-"
"workspace-{core,alloc,std}` are patched with a `path` source to the "
"directory for the corresponding crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:214
msgid ""
"Historically, there have necessarily been C dependencies of the standard "
"library, increasing the complexity of the build environment required. While "
"these have largely been removed over time - for example, `libbacktrace` "
"previously depended on `backtrace-sys` but now uses `gimli` ([rust#46439]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/46439)), a pure-rust implementation. "
"There are still some C dependencies:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:220
msgid ""
"`libunwind` will either link to the LLVM `libunwind` or the system's "
"`libunwind`/`libgcc_s`. LLVM's `libunwind` is shipped as part of the rustup "
"component for the standard library and will be linked against when `-Clink-"
"self-contained` is used"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:224
msgid "This only applies to Linux and Fuchsia targets"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:225
msgid ""
"`compiler_builtins` has an optional `c` feature that will use optimised "
"routines from `compiler-rt` when enabled. It is enabled for the pre-built "
"standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:228
msgid ""
"`compiler_builtins` has an optional `mem` feature that provides symbols for "
"common memory routines (e.g. `memcpy`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:230
msgid ""
"It is enabled automatically on some `no_std` platforms as when `std` is "
"built `libc` provides these routines."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:232
msgid ""
"Users can rely on weak linkage to override these symbols, but in scenarios "
"where weak linkage is not supported or where the symbols are to be "
"overridden from a shared library, then users must directly turn the feature "
"off."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:236
msgid ""
"To use sanitizers, the sanitizer runtimes from LLVM's compiler-rt need to be "
"linked against. Building of these is enabled in `bootstrap.toml` ([`build."
"sanitizers`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/"
"d13a431a6cc69cd65efe7c3eb7808251d6fd7a46/bootstrap.example.toml#L388)) and "
"they are included in the rustup components shipped by the project."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:241
msgid ""
"Dependencies of the standard library may use unstable or internal compiler "
"and language features only when they are a dependency of the standard "
"library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:244
msgid "Features"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:247
msgid ""
"There are a handful of features defined in the standard library crates' "
"`Cargo.toml`s. These features are not strictly additive (`llvm-libunwind` "
"and `system-llvm-libunwind` are mutually exclusive). There is currently no "
"stable existing mechanism for users to enable or disable these features. The "
"default set of features is determined by [logic in bootstrap](https://github."
"com/rust-lang/rust/blob/00b526212bbdd68872d6f964fcc9a14a66c36fd8/src/"
"bootstrap/src/lib.rs#L732) and [the `rust.std-features` key in `bootstrap."
"toml`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"blob/00b526212bbdd68872d6f964fcc9a14a66c36fd8/bootstrap.example.toml#L816). "
"The enabled features are often different depending on the target."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:255
msgid ""
"It is also common for user crates to depend on the standard library (via `#!"
"[no_std]`) conditional on Cargo features being enabled or disabled (e.g. a "
"`std` feature or if `--test` is used)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:259
msgid "Target support"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:262
msgid ""
"The `std` crate's [`build.rs`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/"
"f315e6145802e091ff9fceab6db627a4b4ec2b86/library/std/build.rs#L17) checks "
"for supported values of the `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_*` environment variables. "
"These variables are akin to the conditional compilation [configuration "
"options](https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/conditional-compilation."
"html#set-configuration-options), and often correspond to parts of the target "
"triple (for example, `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_OS` corresponds to the \"os\" part of "
"a target triple - \"linux\" in \"aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu\"). This "
"filtering is strict enough to distinguish between built-in targets but loose "
"enough to match similar custom targets. There is no equivalent mechanism on "
"the `alloc` or `core` crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:271
msgid ""
"When encountering an unknown or unsupported operating system then the "
"`restricted_std` cfg is set. `restricted_std` marks the entire standard "
"library as unstable, requiring `feature(restricted_std)` to be enabled on "
"any crate that depends on it. The only way for users to enable the "
"`restricted_std` feature on behalf of dependencies is the uncommon `-Zcrate-"
"attr=features(restricted_std)` rustc flag and users commonly report that "
"they are not aware how to do this."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:278
msgid ""
"Cargo and rustc support custom targets, defined in JSON files according to "
"an unstable schema defined in the compiler. On nightly, users can dump the "
"target-spec-json for an existing target using `--print target-spec-json`. "
"This JSON can be saved in a file, tweaked and used as the argument to `--"
"target`. It is unintentional but custom target specifications can be used "
"with `--target` even on stable toolchains ([rust#71009](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/rust/issues/71009) proposes destabilising this behaviour). "
"However, as custom targets do not have a pre-built standard library and so "
"must use `-Zbuild-std`, their use is relegated to nightly toolchains in "
"practice. Custom targets may have `restricted_std` set depending on their "
"`cfg` configuration options."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:289
msgid "Prelude"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:292
msgid ""
"rustc has the concept of the \"extern prelude\" which is the set of crates "
"that can be referred to without an explicit `extern crate` statement. "
"Originally this was populated by users writing `extern crate $crate` in "
"their code for each direct dependency. Since the 2018 edition, crates passed "
"via `--extern` are added to the extern prelude. `core` is always added to "
"the extern prelude. For crates without the `#![no_std]` attribute, `std` is "
"added to the extern prelude."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:299
msgid ""
"`core` or `std`'s prelude module (depending on the presence of `#![no_std]`) "
"is imported by rustc injecting a `use $crate::prelude::rust_20XX::*` "
"statement."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:302
msgid ""
"`extern crate` can still be used and will search for the dependency in "
"locations where direct dependencies can be found, such as `-L crate=` paths "
"or in the sysroot. `-L dependency=` paths will not be searched, as these "
"directories only contain indirect dependencies (i.e. dependencies of direct "
"dependencies)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:307
msgid ""
"Although only `std` or `core` are added to the extern prelude automatically, "
"users can still write `extern crate alloc` or `extern crate test` to load "
"them from the sysroot."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:311
msgid ""
"`--extern` has a `noprelude` modifier which will allow the user to use `--"
"extern` to specify the location at which a crate can be found without adding "
"it to the extern prelude. This could allow a path for crates like `alloc` or "
"`test` to be provided without affecting the observable behaviour of the "
"language."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:317
msgid "Panic strategies"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:320
msgid ""
"Rust has the concept of a _panic handler_, which is a crate that is "
"responsible for performing a panic. There are various panic handler crates "
"on crates.io, such as [panic-abort](https://crates.io/crates/panic-abort) "
"(which is different from the `panic_abort` panic runtime!), [panic-halt]"
"(https://crates.io/crates/panic-halt), [panic-itm](https://crates.io/crates/"
"panic-itm), and [panic-semihosting](https://crates.io/crates/panic-"
"semihosting). Panic handler crates define a function annotated with "
"`#[panic_handler]`. There can only be one `#[panic_handler]` in the crate "
"graph."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:327
msgid ""
"`core` uses the panic handler to implement panics inserted by code "
"generation (e.g. arithmetic overflow or out-of-bounds access) and the `core::"
"panic!` macro immediately delegates to the panic handler crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:331
msgid ""
"`std` defines a panic handler. `std`'s panic handler function and its `std::"
"panic!` macro print panic information to stderr and delegate to a _panic "
"runtime_ to decide what to do next, determined by the _panic strategy_."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:335
msgid ""
"There are two panic runtime crates in the standard library - `panic_unwind` "
"(which gracefully unwinds the stack using `libunwind` and performs cleanup) "
"and `panic_abort` (which terminates the program shortly after being called). "
"Each target supported by rustc specifies a default panic strategy - either "
"\"unwind\" or \"abort\" - though these are only relevant if `std`'s panic "
"handler is used (i.e. the target isn't a `no_std` target or being used with "
"a `no_std` crate)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:342
msgid ""
"Rust's `-Cpanic` flag allows the user to choose the panic strategy, with the "
"target's default as a fallback. If `-Cpanic=unwind` is provided then this "
"doesn't guarantee that the unwind strategy is used, as the target may not "
"support it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:347
msgid ""
"Both crates are compiled and shipped with the pre-built standard library for "
"targets which support `std`. Some targets have a pre-built standard library "
"with only the `core` and `alloc` crates, such as the `x86_64-unknown-none` "
"target. While `x86_64-unknown-none` defaults to the `abort` panic strategy, "
"as this target does not support the standard library, this default isn't "
"actually relevant."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:354
msgid ""
"The `std` crate has a `panic_unwind` feature that enables an optional "
"dependency on the `panic_unwind` crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:357
msgid ""
"`core` also provides support for the (unstable) `-Cpanic=immediate_abort` "
"strategy by modifying the `core::panic!` macro to immediately call the abort "
"intrinsic without calling the panic handler, which can dramatically reduce "
"code size. `std` also adds an immediate abort to its `panic!` macro."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:362
msgid "Cargo"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:365
msgid ""
"Cargo's building of the dependency graph is largely driven by the registry "
"index, except for crates from `git` or `path` sources."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:368
msgid ""
"[Cargo registries](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/nightly-rustc/cargo/"
"sources/registry/index.html), like crates.io, are centralised sources for "
"crates. A registry's index is the interface between Cargo and the registry "
"that Cargo queries to know which versions are available for any given crate, "
"what its dependencies are, etc."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:373
msgid ""
"Cargo can query registries using a Git protocol which caches the registry on "
"disk, or using a sparse protocol which exposes the index over HTTP and "
"allows Cargo to avoid having a local copy of the whole index, which has "
"become quite large for crates.io."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:378
msgid ""
"crates.io's registry index is exposed as both a HTTP API and a Git "
"repository - [rust-lang/crates.io-index](https://github.com/rust-lang/crates."
"io-index) - both are updated automatically by crates.io when crates are "
"published, yanked, etc. The HTTP API is mostly used."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:382
msgid ""
"Each crate in the registry index has a JSON file, following [a defined "
"schema](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/registry-index.html#json-"
"schema) which is jointly maintained by the Cargo and crates.io teams. Crates "
"may refer to those in other registries, but all non-`path`/`git` crates in "
"the dependency graph must exist in a registry. As the registry index drives "
"the building of Cargo's dependency graph, all non-`path`/`git` crates that "
"end up in the dependency graph must be present in a registry."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:390
msgid ""
"When a package is published, Cargo posts a JSON blob to the registry which "
"is not an index entry but has sufficient information to generate one. crates."
"io does not use Cargo's JSON blob, instead re-generating it from the `Cargo."
"toml` (this avoids the index and `Cargo.toml` from going out-of-sync due to "
"bugs or malicious publishes). As a consequence, changes to the index format "
"must be duplicated in Cargo and crates.io. Behind the scenes, data from the "
"`Cargo.toml` extracted by crates.io is written to a database, which is where "
"the index entry and frontend are generated from."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:399
msgid ""
"Dependency information of crates in the registry are rendered in the crates."
"io frontend."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:402
msgid ""
"Registries can have different policies for what crates are accepted. For "
"example, crates.io does not permit publishing packages named `std` or `core` "
"but other registries might."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:406
msgid "Public/private dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:409
msgid ""
"[Public and private dependencies](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/44663) are an unstable feature which enables declaring which "
"dependencies form part of a library's public interface, so as to make it "
"easier to avoid breaking semver compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:413
msgid ""
"With the `public-dependency` feature enabled, dependencies are marked as "
"\"private\" by default which can be overridden with a `public = true` "
"declaration."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:416
msgid ""
"Private dependencies are passed to rustc with a `priv` modifier to the `--"
"extern` flag. Dependencies without this modifier are treated as public by "
"rustc for backwards compatibility reasons. rust emits the `exported-private-"
"dependencies` lint if an item from a private dependency is re-exported."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:422
msgid "Target modifiers"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:425
msgid ""
"[rfcs#3716](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3716-target-modifiers.html) "
"introduced the concept of _target modifiers_ to rustc. Flags marked as "
"target modifiers must match across the entire crate graph or the compilation "
"will fail."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:429
msgid ""
"For example, flags are made target modifiers when they change the ABI of "
"generated code and could result in unsound ABI mismatches if two crates are "
"linked together with different values of the flag set."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:433
msgid "History"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:436
msgid ""
"_The following summary of the prior art is necessarily less detailed than "
"the source material, which is exhaustively surveyed in [Appendix: Exhaustive "
"literature review](https://hackmd.io/@davidtwco/BJG0jgZkbl)._"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:440
msgid "[rfcs#1133](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/1133) (2015)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:443
msgid ""
"build-std was first proposed in a [2015 RFC (rfcs#1133)](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/rfcs/pull/1133) by [Ericson2314](https://github.com/Ericson2314), "
"aiming to improve support for targets that do not have a pre-built standard "
"library; to enable building the standard library with different profiles; "
"and to simplify `rustbuild` (now `bootstrap`). It also was written with the "
"goal of supporting the user in providing a custom implementation of the "
"standard library and supporting different implementations of the language "
"that provide their own standard libraries."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:451
msgid ""
"This RFC proposed that the standard library be made an explicit dependency "
"in `Cargo.toml` and be rebuilt automatically when required. An implicit "
"dependency on the standard library would be added automatically unless an "
"explicit dependency is written. This RFC was written prior to a stable `#!"
"[no_std]` attribute and so does not address the circumstance where an "
"implicit dependency would make a `#![no_std]` crate fail to compile on a "
"target that does not support the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:459
msgid ""
"There were objectives of and possibilities enabled by the RFC that were not "
"shared with the project teams at the time, such as the standard library "
"being a regular crate on crates.io and the concept of the sysroot being "
"retired. Despite this, the RFC appeared to be close to acceptance before "
"being blocked by Cargo having a mechanism to have unstable features and then "
"closed in favour of [cargo#4959](https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/"
"issues/4959)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:466
msgid ""
"[xargo](https://github.com/japaric/xargo) and [cargo#4959](https://github."
"com/rust-lang/cargo/issues/4959) (2016)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:469
msgid ""
"While the discussions around [rfcs#1133](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/1133) were ongoing, [xargo](https://github.com/japaric/xargo) was "
"released in 2016. Xargo is a Cargo wrapper that builds a sysroot with a "
"customised standard library and then uses that with regular Cargo operations "
"(i.e. `xargo build` performs the same operation as `cargo build` but with a "
"customised standard library). Configuration for the customised standard "
"library was configured in the `Xargo.toml`, supporting configuring codegen "
"flags, profile settings, Cargo features and multi-stage builds. It required "
"nightly to build the standard library as it did not use `RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP`. "
"Xargo had inherent limitations due to being a Cargo wrapper, leading to "
"suggestions that its functionality be integrated into Cargo."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:480
msgid ""
"[cargo#4959](https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/issues/4959) is a proposal "
"inspired by [xargo](https://github.com/japaric/xargo), suggesting that a "
"`[sysroot]` section be added to `.cargo/config` which would enable similar "
"configuration to that of `Xargo.toml`. If this configuration is set, Cargo "
"would build and use a sysroot with a customised standard library according "
"to the configuration specified and the release profile. This sysroot would "
"be rebuilt whenever relevant configuration changes (e.g. profiles). "
"[cargo#4959](https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/issues/4959) received varied "
"feedback: the proposed syntax was not sufficiently user-friendly; it did not "
"enable the user to customise the standard library implementation; and that "
"exposing bootstrap stages was brittle and user-unfriendly. [cargo#4959]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/issues/4959) wasn't updated after "
"submission so ultimately stalled and remains open."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:491
msgid ""
"[rfcs#1133](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/1133) and [cargo#4959]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/issues/4959) took very different "
"approaches to build-std, with [cargo#4959](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"cargo/issues/4959) proposing a simpler approach that exposed the necessary "
"low-level machinery to users and [rfcs#1133](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"rfcs/pull/1133) attempting to take a more first-class and user-friendly "
"approach that has many tricky design implications."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:496
msgid "[rfcs#2663](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/2663) (2019)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:499
msgid ""
"In 2019, [_rfcs#2663: `std` Aware Cargo_](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/2663) was opened as the most recent RFC attempting to advance build-"
"std. [rfcs#2663](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/2663) shared many of "
"the motivations of [rfcs#1133](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/1133): "
"building the standard library for tier three and custom targets; customising "
"the standard library with different Cargo features; and applying different "
"codegen flags to the standard library. It did not concern itself with build-"
"std's potential use in `rustbuild` or with abolishing the sysroot."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:507
msgid ""
"[rfcs#2663](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/2663) was primarily "
"concerned with what functionality should be available to the user and what "
"the user experience ought to be. It proposed that `core`, `alloc` and `std` "
"be automatically built when the target did not have a pre-built standard "
"library available through rustup. It would be automatically rebuilt on any "
"target when the profile configuration was modified such that it no longer "
"matched the pre-built standard library. If using nightly, the user could "
"enable Cargo features and modify the source of the standard library. "
"Standard library dependencies were implicit by default, as today, but would "
"be written explicitly when enabling Cargo features. It also aimed to "
"stabilise the target-spec-json format and allow \"stable\" Cargo features to "
"be enabled on stable toolchains, and as such proposed the concept of stable "
"and unstable Cargo features be introduced."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:520
msgid ""
"There was a lot of feedback on [rfcs#2663](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/2663) which largely stemmed from it being very high-level, containing "
"many large unresolved questions and details left for the implementers to "
"work out. For example, it proposed that there be a concept of stable and "
"unstable Cargo features but did not elaborate any further, leaving that as "
"an implementation detail. Nevertheless, the proposal was valuable in more "
"clearly elucidating a potential user experience that build-std could aim "
"for, and the feedback provided was incorporated into the [wg-cargo-std-aware]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware) effort, described below."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:529
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware) (2019-)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:532
msgid ""
"[rfcs#2663](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/2663) demonstrated that "
"there was demand for a mechanism for being able to (re-)build the standard "
"library, and the feedback showed that this was a thorny problem with lots of "
"complexity, so in 2019, the [wg-cargo-std-aware](https://github.com/rust-"
"lang/wg-cargo-std-aware) repository was created to organise related work and "
"explore the issues involved in build-std."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:538
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware) led to "
"the current unstable implementation of `-Zbuild-std` in Cargo, which is "
"described in detail in the [_Implementation summary_ section]"
"(#implementation-summary) below."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:542
msgid ""
"Issues in the wg-cargo-std-aware repository can be roughly partitioned into "
"seven categories:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:545
msgid "**Exploring the motivations and use cases for the standard library**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:547
msgid ""
"There are a handful of motivations catalogued in the [wg-cargo-std-aware]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware) repository, corresponding "
"to those raised in the earlier RFCs and proposals:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:550
msgid ""
"Building with custom profile settings ([wg-cargo-std-aware#2](https://github."
"com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/2))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:551
msgid ""
"Building for unsupported targets ([wg-cargo-std-aware#3](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/3))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:552
msgid ""
"Building with different Cargo features ([wg-cargo-std-aware#4](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/4))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:553
msgid ""
"Replacing the source of the standard library ([wg-cargo-std-aware#7](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/7))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:554
msgid ""
"Using build-std in bootstrap/rustbuild ([wg-cargo-std-aware#19](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/19))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:555
msgid ""
"Improving the user experience for `no_std` binary projects ([wg-cargo-std-"
"aware#36](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/36))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:558
msgid ""
"These are all either fairly self-explanatory, described in the summary of "
"the previous RFCs/proposals above, or in the [_Motivation_](#motivation) "
"section of this RFC."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:562
msgid "**Support for build-std in Cargo's subcommands**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:564
msgid ""
"Cargo has various subcommands where the desired behaviour when used with "
"build-std needs some thought and consideration. A handful of issues were "
"created to track this, most receiving little to no discussion: [`cargo "
"metadata`](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/20), "
"[`cargo clean`](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/21), "
"[`cargo pkgid`](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/24), "
"and [the `-p` flag](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/26)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:570
msgid ""
"[`cargo fetch`](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/22) "
"had fairly intuitive interactions with build-std - that `cargo fetch` should "
"also fetch any dependencies of the standard library - which was implemented "
"in [cargo#10129](https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/pull/10129)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:574
msgid ""
"The [`--build-plan` flag](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/45) does not support build-std and its issue did not receive much "
"discussion, but the future of this flag in its entirety seems to be "
"uncertain."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:578
msgid ""
"[`cargo vendor`](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/23) "
"did receive lots of discussion. Vendoring the standard library is desirable "
"(for the same reasons as any vendoring), but would lock the user to a "
"specific version of the toolchain when using a vendored standard library. "
"However, if the `rust-src` component contained already-vendored "
"dependencies, then `cargo vendor` would not need to support build-std and "
"users would see the same advantages."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:585
msgid ""
"Vendored standard library dependencies were implemented using a hacky "
"approach (necessarily, prior to the standard library having its own "
"workspace), but this was later reverted due to bugs. No attempt has been "
"made to reimplement vendoring since the standard library has had its own "
"workspace."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:591
msgid "**Dependencies of the standard library**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:593
msgid ""
"There are a handful of dependencies of the standard library that may pose "
"challenges for build-std by dint of needing a working C toolchain or special-"
"casing."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:597
msgid ""
"[`libbacktrace`](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/16) "
"previously required a C compiler to build `backtrace-sys`, but now uses "
"`gimli` internally."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:600
msgid ""
"[`compiler_builtins`](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/15) has a `c` feature that uses C versions of some intrinsics that "
"are more optimised. This is used by the pre-built standard library, and if "
"not used by build-std, could be a point of divergence. `compiler-builtins/c` "
"can have a significant impact on code quality and build size. It also has a "
"`mem` feature which provides symbols (`memcpy`, etc) for platforms without "
"`std` that don't have these same symbols provided by `libc`. "
"`compiler_builtins` is also built with a large number of compilation units "
"to force each function into a different unit, avoiding unintentionally "
"bringing in a symbol that conflicts with one in the system's `libgcc`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:611
msgid ""
"['unwind'](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/29) links "
"to the system's version of libunwind. Enabling the `llvm-libunwind` feature, "
"`-Clink-self-contained` or `-Ctarget-feature=+crt-static` will statically "
"link to the pre-built `libunwind` distributed in the standard library "
"component for the target, if present."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:617
msgid ""
"[Sanitizers](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/17), "
"when enabled, require a sanitizer runtime to be present. These are currently "
"built by bootstrap and part of LLVM."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:621
msgid "**Design considerations**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:623
msgid ""
"There are many design considerations discussed in the [wg-cargo-std-aware]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware) repository:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:626
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#5](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/5) explored how/if dependencies on the standard library should be "
"declared. The issue claims that users should have to opt-in to build-std, "
"support alternative standard library implementations, and that Cargo needs "
"to be able to pass `--extern` to rustc for all dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:631
msgid ""
"It is an open question how to handle multiple dependencies each declaring a "
"dependency on the standard library. A preference towards unifying standard "
"library dependencies was expressed (these would have no concept of a "
"version, so just union all features)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:636
msgid ""
"There was no consensus on how to find a balance between explicitly depending "
"on the standard library versus implicitly, or on whether the pre-built-ness "
"of a dependency should be surfaced to the user."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:640
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#6](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/6) argues that target-spec-json would be de-facto stable if it can be "
"used by build-std on stable. While `--target=custom.json` can be used on "
"stable today, it effectively requires build-std and so a nightly toolchain. "
"As build-std enables custom targets to be used on stable, this would "
"effectively be a greater commitment to the current stability of custom "
"targets than currently exists and would warrant an explicit decision."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:647
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#8](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/8) highlighted that a more-portable standard library would be "
"beneficial for build-std (i.e. a `std` that could build on any target), but "
"that making the standard library more portable isn't necessarily in-scope "
"for build-std."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:652
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#11](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/11) investigated how build-std could get the standard library "
"sources. rustup can download `rust-src`, but there was a preference "
"expressed that rustup not be required. Cargo could have reasonable default "
"probing locations that could be used by distros and would include where "
"rustup puts `rust-src`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:658
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#12](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/12) concluded that the `Cargo.lock` of the standard library would "
"need to be respected so that the project can guarantee that the standard "
"library works with the project's current testing."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:662
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#13](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/13) explored how to determine the default set of cfg values for the "
"standard library. This is currently computed by bootstrap. This could be "
"duplicated in Cargo in the short-term, made visible to build-std through "
"some configuration, or require the user to explicitly declare them."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:668
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#14](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/14) looks into additional rustc flags and environment variables "
"passed by bootstrap to the compiler. A comparison of the compilation flags "
"from bootstrap and build-std was [posted in a comment](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/14#issuecomment-2315878717). No "
"solutions were suggested, other than that it may need a similar mechanism as "
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#13](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/13)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:675
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#29](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/29) tries to determine how to support different panic strategies. "
"Should Cargo use the profile to decide what to use? How does it know which "
"panic strategy crate to use? It is argued that Cargo ought to work "
"transparently - if the user sets the panic strategy differently then a "
"rebuild is triggered."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:681
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#30](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/30) identifies that some targets have special handling in bootstrap "
"which will need to be duplicated in build-std. Targets could be allowlisted "
"or denylisted to avoid having to address this initially."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:685
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#38](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/38) argues that a forced lock of the standard library is desirable, "
"to which there was no disagreement. This was more relevant when build-std "
"did not use the on-disk `Cargo.lock`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:689
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#39](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/39) explores the interaction between build-std and public/private "
"dependencies ([rfcs#3516](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3516-public-"
"private-dependencies.html)). Should the standard library always be public? "
"There were no solutions presented, only that if defined in `Cargo.toml`, the "
"standard library will likely inherit the default from that."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:694
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#43](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/43) investigates the options for the UX of build-std. `-Zbuild-std` "
"flag is not a good experience as it needs added to every invocation and has "
"few extension points. Using build-std should be an unstable feature at "
"first. It was argued that build-std should be transparent and happen "
"automatically when Cargo determines it is necessary. There are concerns that "
"this could trigger too often and that it should only happen automatically "
"for ABI-modifying flags."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:702
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#46](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/46) observes that some targets link against special object flags (e."
"g. `crt1.o` on musl) and that build-std will need to handle these without "
"hardcoding target-specific logic. There were no conclusions, but `-Clink-"
"self-contained` might be able to help."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:707
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#47](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/47) discusses how to handle targets that typically ship with a "
"different linker (e.g. `rust-lld` or `gcc`). `rust-lld` is now shipped by "
"default reducing the potential impact of this, though it is discovered via "
"the sysroot, and so will need to be found via another mechanism if disabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:712
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#50](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/50) argues that the impact on build probes ought to be considered and "
"was later closed as t-cargo do not want to support build probes."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:716
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#51](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/51) plans for removal of `rustc-dep-of-std`, identifying that if "
"explicit dependencies on the standard library are adopted, that the need for "
"this feature could be made redundant."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:720
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#68](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/68) notices that `profiler_builtins` needs to be compiled after "
"`core` (i.e. `core` can't be compiled with profiling). The error message has "
"been improved for this but there was otherwise no commentary. This has "
"changed since the issue was filed, as `profiler_builtins` is now a `#!"
"[no_core]` crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:726
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#85](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/85) considers that there has to be a deliberate testing strategy in "
"place between the [rust-lang/rust](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust) and "
"[rust-lang/cargo](https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo) repositories to ensure "
"there is no breakage. `rust-toolstate` could be used but is not very good. "
"Alternatively, Cargo could become a [JOSH](https://josh-project.github.io/"
"josh/intro.html) subtree of [rust-lang/rust](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"rust)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:732
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#86](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/86) proposes that the initial set of targets supported by build-std "
"be limited at first to further reduce scope and limit exposure to the "
"trickier issues."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:736
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#88](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/88) reports that `cargo doc -Zbuild-std` doesn't generate links to "
"the standard library. Cargo doesn't think the standard library comes from "
"crates.io, and bootstrap isn't involved to pass `-Zcrate-"
"attr=\"doc(html_root_url=..)\"` like in the pre-built standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:742
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#90](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/90) asks how `restricted_std` should apply to custom targets. "
"`restricted_std` is triggered based on the `target_os` value, which means it "
"will apply for some custom targets but not others. build-std needs to "
"determine what guarantees are desirable/expected. Current implementation "
"wants slightly-modified-from-default target specs to be accepted and "
"completely new target specs to hit `restricted_std`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:749
msgid ""
"[wg-cargo-std-aware#92](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/92) suggests that some targets could be made \"unstable\" and as such "
"only support build-std on nightly. This forces users of those targets to use "
"nightly where they will receive more frequent fixes for their target. It "
"would also permit more experimentation with build-std while enabling "
"stabilisation for mainstream targets."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:755
msgid ""
"**Implementation considerations** These won't be discussed in this summary, "
"see [the implementation summary](#implementation-summary) or [the relevant "
"section of the literature review for more detail](https://hackmd.io/"
"@davidtwco/BJG0jgZkbl#implementation)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:759
msgid ""
"**Bugs in the compiler or standard library** These aren't especially "
"relevant to this summary, see [the relevant section of the literature review "
"for more detail](https://hackmd.io/@davidtwco/BJG0jgZkbl#bugs-in-the-"
"compiler-or-standard-library)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:763
msgid ""
"**Cargo feature requests narrowly applied to build-std** These aren't "
"especially relevant to this summary, see [the relevant section of the "
"literature review for more detail](https://hackmd.io/@davidtwco/"
"BJG0jgZkbl#cargo-feature-requests-narrowly-applied-to-build-std)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:767
msgid ""
"Since around 2020, activity in the [wg-cargo-std-aware](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware) repository largely trailed off and there have "
"not been any significant developments related to build-std since."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:771
msgid "Implementation summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:774
msgid ""
"_An exhaustive review of implementation-related issues, pull requests and "
"discussions can be found in [the relevant section of the literature review]"
"(https://hackmd.io/@davidtwco/BJG0jgZkbl#implementation)._"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:778
msgid ""
"There has been an unstable and experimental implementation of build-std in "
"Cargo since August 2019 ([wg-cargo-std-aware#10](https://github.com/rust-"
"lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/10)/[cargo#7216](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"cargo/pull/7216))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:781
msgid ""
"[cargo#7216](https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/pull/7216) added the [`-"
"Zbuild-std`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/unstable.html#build-"
"std) flag to Cargo. `-Zbuild-std` re-builds the standard library crates "
"which rustc then uses instead of the pre-built standard library from the "
"sysroot."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:785
msgid ""
"Originally, `-Zbuild-std` always build `std` by default. Since the addition "
"of the `std` field to target metadata in [rust#122305](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/rust/pull/122305), Cargo only builds `std` by default if `metadata."
"std` is true."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:789
msgid ""
"`test` is also built if `std` is being built and tests are being run with "
"the default harness."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:792
msgid ""
"Optionally, users can provide the list of crates to be built, though this "
"was intended as an escape hatch to work around bugs - the arguments to the "
"flag are unstable since the names of crates comprising the standard library "
"are not stable."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:797
msgid ""
"Cargo has a hardcoded list of what dependencies need to be added for a given "
"user-requested crate (i.e. `std` implies building `core`, `alloc`, "
"`compiler_builtins`, etc.). It is common for users to manually specify the "
"`panic_abort` crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:802
msgid ""
"Originally, `-Zbuild-std` required that `--target` be provided ([wg-cargo-"
"std-aware#25](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/25)) to "
"force Cargo to use different sysroots for the host and target , but this "
"restriction was later resolved ([cargo#14317](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"cargo/pull/14317))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:806
msgid ""
"A second flag, [`-Zbuild-std-features`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/"
"reference/unstable.html#build-std-features), was added in [cargo#8490]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/pull/8490) and allows overriding the "
"default Cargo features of the standard library. Like the arguments to `-"
"Zbuild-std`, the values accepted by this flag are inherently unstable as the "
"library team has not committed to any of the standard library's Cargo "
"features being stable. Features are enabled on the `sysroot` crate and "
"propagate down through the crate graph of the standard library (e.g. "
"`compiler-builtins-mem` is a feature in `sysroot`, `std`, `alloc`, and "
"`core` until `compiler_builtins`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:815
msgid ""
"build-std gets the source of the standard library from the `rust-src` rustup "
"component. This does not happen automatically and the user must ensure the "
"component has been downloaded themselves. Only the standard library crates "
"from the [rust-lang/rust](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust) repository are "
"included in the `rust-src` dependency (i.e. none of the crates.io "
"dependencies)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:821
msgid ""
"When `-Zbuild-std` has been passed, Cargo creates a second workspace for the "
"standard library based on the `Cargo.{toml,lock}` from the `rust-src` "
"component. Originally this was an in-memory workspace, prior to the standard "
"library having a separate workspace from the compiler which could be used "
"independently ([rust#128534](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/128534)/"
"[cargo#14358](https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/pull/14358)). This "
"workspace is then resolved separately and the resolve is combined with the "
"user's resolve to produce a dependency graph of things to build with the "
"user's crates depending on the standard library's crates. Some additional "
"work is done to deduplicate crates across the graph and then this crate "
"graph is used to drive work (usually rustc invocations) as usual. This "
"approach allows for build-time parallelism and sharing of crates between the "
"two separate resolves but does involve `build-std`\\-specific logic in and "
"around unit generation and is very unlike the rest of Cargo ([wg-cargo-std-"
"aware#64](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/64))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:835
msgid ""
"Resolving the standard library separately from the user's crate helps "
"guarantee that the exact dependency versions of the pre-built standard "
"library are used, which is a key constraint ([wg-cargo-std-aware#12](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/12)). Locking the standard "
"library could also help ([wg-cargo-std-aware#38](https://github.com/rust-"
"lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/38)). A consequence of this is that each of "
"the Cargo subcommands (e.g. `cargo metadata`) need to have special support "
"for build-std implemented, but this might be desirable."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:842
msgid ""
"The standard library crates are considered non-local packages and so are not "
"compiled with incremental compilation or dep-info fingerprint tracking and "
"any warnings will be silenced."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:846
msgid ""
"build-std provides newly-built standard library dependencies to rustc using "
"`--extern noprelude:$crate`. `noprelude` was added in [rust#67074](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/67074) to support build-std and ensure that "
"loading from the sysroot and using `--extern` were equivalent ([wg-cargo-std-"
"aware#40](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/40)). Prior "
"to the addition of `noprelude`, build-std briefly created new sysroots and "
"used those instead of `--extern` ([cargo#7421](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"cargo/pull/7421)). rustc can still try to load a crate from the sysroot if "
"the user uses it which is currently a common source of confusing \"duplicate "
"lang item\" errors (as the user ends up with build-std `core` and sysroot "
"`core` conflicting)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:856
msgid ""
"Host dependencies like build scripts and `proc_macro` crates use the "
"existing pre-built standard library from the sysroot, so Cargo does not pass "
"`--extern` to those."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:860
msgid ""
"Modifications to the standard library are not supported. While build-std has "
"no mechanism to detect or prevent modifications to the `rust-src` content, "
"rebuilds aren't triggered automatically on modifications. The user cannot "
"override dependencies in the standard library workspace with `[patch]` "
"sections of their `Cargo.toml`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:866
msgid ""
"To simplify build-std in Cargo, build-std wants to be able to always build "
"`std`, which is accomplished through use of the [`unsupported` module in "
"`std`'s platform abstraction layer](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/"
"f768dc01da9a681716724418ccf64ce55bd396c5/library/std/src/sys/pal/mod.rs#L68-"
"L69), and `restricted_std`. `std` checks for unsupported targets in its "
"[`build.rs`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/"
"f315e6145802e091ff9fceab6db627a4b4ec2b86/library/std/build.rs#L17) and "
"applies the `restricted_std` cfg which marks the standard library as "
"unstable for unsupported targets."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:873
msgid ""
"Users can enable the `restricted_std` feature in their crates. This "
"mechanism has been noted as confusing ([wg-cargo-std-aware#87](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/87)) and has the issue that "
"the user cannot opt into the feature on behalf of dependencies ([wg-cargo-"
"std-aware#69](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/69))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:878
msgid ""
"The initial implementation does not include support for build-std in many of "
"Cargo's subcommands including `metadata`, `clean`, `vendor`, `pkgid` and the "
"`-p` options for various commands. Support for `cargo fetch` was implemented "
"in [cargo#10129](https://github.com/rust-lang/cargo/pull/10129)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:883
msgid "`no_std` Usability"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:886
msgid "There are also issues related to the usability of `no_std` crates:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:888
msgid ""
"Discoverability of `no_std` crates is difficult with a mix of categories "
"(`no-std`) and keywords (`nostd`/`no_std`) that are not used consistently by "
"`no_std` crates ([crates.io#7306](https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io/"
"pull/7306))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:892
msgid ""
"`no_std` crates can accidentally and easily depend on crates that use `std` "
"which can result in build failures in some targets ([cargo#8798](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/cargo/issues/8798))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:895
msgid "Related work"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:898
msgid ""
"There are a variety of ongoing efforts, ideas, RFCs or draft notes "
"describing features that are related or would be beneficial for build-std:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:901
msgid ""
"**[Opaque dependencies](https://hackmd.io/@epage/ByGfPtRell)**, [epage]"
"(https://github.com/epage), May 2025"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:902
msgid ""
"Introduces the concept of an opaque dependency that has its own `Cargo."
"lock`, `RUSTFLAGS` and `profile`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:904
msgid ""
"Opaque dependencies could enable a variety of build-time performance "
"improvements:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:906
msgid ""
"Caching - differences in dependency versions can cause unique instances of "
"every dependent crate"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:908
msgid ""
"Pre-built binaries - can leverage a pre-built artifact for a given opaque "
"dependency"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:910
msgid "e.g. the standard library's distributed `rlib`s"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:911
msgid ""
"MIR-only/cross-crate lazy compilation - Small dependencies could be built "
"lazily and larger dependencies built once"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:913
msgid ""
"Optimising dependencies - dependencies could always be optimised when they "
"are unlikely to be needed during debugging"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:916
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:919
msgid "\\[!IMPORTANT\\]"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:921
msgid ""
"This section lists all of the motivations that have been associated with "
"build-std in its various iterations, but not all of these use cases will be "
"addressed by this project goal."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:925
msgid ""
"The motivations that will not be addressed are nevertheless mentioned here "
"so that reviewers have a more complete context for what has and hasn't been "
"desired of build-std over time."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:929
msgid ""
"While the pre-built standard library has been sufficient for the majority of "
"Rust users, there are a variety of use-cases which require the ability to "
"rebuild the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:933
msgid ""
"**Building the standard library without relying on unstable escape hatches**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:935
msgid ""
"While tangential to the core of build-std as a feature, projects like Rust "
"for Linux want to be able to build crates from the standard library using a "
"stable toolchain without relying on escape hatches like `RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP` "
"that the Rust project does not encourage use of"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:940
msgid "It is relatively straightforward to support this, hence its inclusion"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:942
msgid ""
"Cargo's implementation of build-std should be able to re-use whichever "
"mechanism is designed to address this"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:945
msgid "**Building standard library crates that are not shipped for a target**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:947
msgid ""
"Targets which have limited `std` support may wish to use the subsets of the "
"standard library which could work but are not shipped by the project (e.g. "
"`std` on `x86_64-unknown-none`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:951
msgid "**Using the standard library with tier three targets**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:953
msgid ""
"There is no stable mechanism for using the standard library on a tier three "
"target that does not ship a pre-built std"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:956
msgid ""
"While it is common for these targets to not support the `std` crate, they "
"should be able to use `core`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:959
msgid ""
"These users are forced to use nightly and the unstable `-Zbuild-std` feature "
"or third-party tools like [cargo-xbuild](https://github.com/rust-osdev/cargo-"
"xbuild) (formerly [xargo](https://github.com/japaric/xargo))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:962
msgid "**Unblock stabilisation of ABI-modifying compiler flags**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:964
msgid ""
"Any compiler flags which change the ABI cannot currently be stabilised as "
"they would immediately mismatch with the pre-built standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:967
msgid ""
"Without an ability to rebuild the standard library using these flags, it is "
"impossible to use them effectively and safely if stabilised"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:970
msgid ""
"ABI-modifying flags are designated as target modifiers ([rfcs#3716](https://"
"rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3716-target-modifiers.html)/[rust#136966](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/136966)) and require that the same value "
"for the flag is passed to all compilation units"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:974
msgid ""
"Flags which need to be set across the entire crate graph to uphold some "
"property (i.e. enhanced security) are also target modifiers"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:977
msgid "For example: sanitizers, control flow integrity, `-Zfixed-x18`, etc"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:979
msgid ""
"**Re-building the standard library with different codegen flags or profile** "
"([wg-cargo-std-aware#2](https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/"
"issues/2))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:982
msgid ""
"Embedded users need to optimise aggressively for size, due to the limited "
"space available on their target platforms, which can be achieved in Cargo by "
"setting `opt-level = s/z` and `panic = \"abort\"` in their profile. However, "
"these settings will not apply to the pre-built standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:987
msgid ""
"Similarly, when deploying to known environments, use of `target-cpu` or "
"`target-feature` can improve the performance of code generation or allow the "
"use of newer hardware features than the target's baseline provides. As "
"above, these configurations will not apply to the pre-built standard library"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:993
msgid ""
"While the pre-built standard library is built to support debugging without "
"compromising size and performance by setting `debuginfo=1`, this isn't "
"ideal, and building the standard library with the dev profile would provide "
"a better experience when debugging"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:998
msgid ""
"The following use cases are not currently planned as part of this project "
"goal, but could be supported with follow-up RFCs (and any RFCs proposed as "
"part of this goal will attempt to ensure they remain viable as future "
"possibilities):"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1002
msgid "**Using the standard library with custom targets**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1004
msgid ""
"There is no stable mechanism for using the standard library for a custom "
"target (using target-spec-json)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1007
msgid ""
"Like tier three targets, these targets often only support `core` and are "
"forced to use nightly today"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1010
msgid ""
"**Enabling Cargo features for the standard library** ([wg-cargo-std-aware#4]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/4))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1012
msgid ""
"There are opportunities to expose Cargo features from the standard library "
"that would be useful for certain subsets of the Rust users."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1015
msgid ""
"For example, embedded users may want to enable `optimize_for_size` or "
"disable `backtrace` to reduce binary size"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1018
msgid "**Progress towards using miri on a stable toolchain**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1020
msgid ""
"One of the limitations of miri is that it requires building the standard "
"library with specific compiler flags that would not be appropriate for the "
"pre-built standard library, this is part of miri's dependency on nightly to "
"build its own sysroot using [rustc-build-sysroot](https://github.com/"
"ralfjung/rustc-build-sysroot)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1025
msgid ""
"Some use cases are unlikely to be supported by the project unless a new and "
"compelling use-case is presented, and so this project goal may make "
"decisions which make these motivations harder to solve in future:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1029
msgid ""
"**Modifying the source code of the standard library** ([wg-cargo-std-aware#7]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/wg-cargo-std-aware/issues/7))"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1031
msgid ""
"Some platforms require a heavily modified standard library that would not be "
"suitable for upstreaming, such as [Apache's SGX SDK](https://github.com/"
"apache/incubator-teaclave-sgx-sdk) which replaces some standard library and "
"ecosystem crates with forks or custom crates for a custom `x86_64-unknown-"
"linux-sgx` target"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1036
msgid ""
"Similarly, some tier three targets may wish to patch standard library "
"dependencies to add or improve support for the target"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1039
msgid ""
"If a stable mechanism were provided to make such changes to the standard "
"library, then this would constrain future standard library development. "
"These changes are better attempted by maintaining a fork of the standard "
"library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1044
msgid "**Retire the concept of the sysroot**"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1046
msgid ""
"Earlier proposals for build-std were motivated in-part by the desire to see "
"the concept of the sysroot retired."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1049
msgid ""
"This is challenging while maintaining backwards-compatibility, especially "
"for users who do not use Cargo and assume rustc can find the standard "
"library in the sysroot. Removing the sysroot has no advantages to the end-"
"user of Rust in itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1054
msgid "Rationale and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1057
msgid ""
"These rationales and alternatives apply to the build-std proposal as a whole:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1059
msgid "Why have rationale sections?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1062
msgid ""
"A separate rationale section makes for easier reading by letting the "
"proposal sections of the RFCs flow better without interruptions or tangents."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1065
msgid "↩ [_Terminology_](#terminology)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1067
msgid "Why not do nothing?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1070
msgid ""
"Support for rebuilding the standard library is a long-standing feature "
"request from subsets of the Rust community and blocks the work of some "
"project teams (e.g. sanitisers and branch protection in the compiler team, "
"amongst others). Inaction forces these users to remain on nightly and depend "
"on the unstable `-Zbuild-std` flag indefinitely. RFCs and discussion dating "
"back to the first stable release of the language demonstrate the longevity "
"of build-std as a need."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1078
msgid "Shouldn't build-std be part of rustup?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1081
msgid ""
"build-std is effectively creating a new sysroot with a customised standard "
"library. rustup as Rust's toolchain manager has existing machinery to create "
"and maintain sysroots, and if it could invoke Cargo to build the standard "
"library then it could create a new toolchain from a build from a `rust-src` "
"component. rustup would be invoking tools from the next layer of abstraction "
"(Cargo) in the same way that Cargo invokes tools from the layer of "
"abstraction after it (rustc)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3873-build-std-context.md:1089
msgid ""
"A brief prototype of this idea was created and a [short design document was "
"drafted](https://hackmd.io/@davidtwco/rkYRlKv_1x) before concluding that it "
"would not be possible. With Cargo's artifact dependencies it may be "
"desirable to build with a different standard library and if rustup was "
"creating different toolchains per-customised standard library then Cargo "
"would need to have knowledge of these to switch between them, which isn't "
"possible (and something of a layering violation). It is also unclear how "
"Cargo would find and use the uncustomized host sysroot for build scripts and "
"procedural macros. In addition rustup's knowledge of sysroots and toolchains "
"is limited to the archives it unpacks - it becoming a part of the build "
"system is not trivial, especially considering it uses a different versioning "
"system to Cargo, Rust and the standard library."
msgstr ""
